// External DRF -- a race-free program must behave sequentially consistently.
//
// rmm-zoo property: edrf (Reasoning guarantees / External DRF).
// Source: Batty et al., "The Problem of Programming Language Concurrency
//         Semantics" (ESOP 2015), which names the guarantee and shows it in
//         tension with permitting thin-air; Boehm & Adve, "Foundations of the
//         C++ Concurrency Memory Model" (PLDI 2008); Moiseenko et al. (2021),
//         Tables 1-2 -- the Reasoning guarantees group.
//
// External DRF: if a program has no data race under SC, the model gives it only
// SC behaviours. This is the positive half. The flag is released and acquired,
// so the accesses to the data location are ordered by happens-before and the
// program is race free; the stale read must therefore be forbidden, as it is
// under SC.
//
// The zoo records edrf = true for MRD, RC11, IMM and 24 others, false for C11,
// Java's ordinary accesses and four more; sMRD has no cell.
data := 0;
f := 0;
{
  data.store(1, rel);
  f.store(1, rel)
} ||| {
  r1 := f.load(acq);
  r2 := data.load(acq)
}
%%
forbid (r1 = 1 && r2 = 0) [RC11]
